 "lang": "en",
 "gap": 0.8,
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "America's second-largest reservoir hit a record low in August. Then the desert flooded."
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Lake Powell, on the Colorado River, fell to its lowest level on record on August fifteenth, according to the Bureau of Reclamation. That left it only about thirty feet above the point where Glen Canyon Dam can no longer make hydropower."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Then came the storms. The Associated Press reports that rain in late October and early November sent floods through normally dry creeks, and hundreds of people were told to evacuate. Since mid-September, the lake has risen more than twenty feet, more than this year's snowmelt added."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "But it is still only about a quarter full. The A.P. says fifteen of seventeen boat launches were unusable last Friday. Reclamation spokesperson Mary Carlson says conditions have improved, but considerable uncertainty remains."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will Lake Powell keep rising this winter? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
